The port is so terrible however, that I encourage everyone interested in this game, or SE's planned ports of FFXIII-2 and Lightning Returns to not get this game until SE improves the quality of this port and includes promises to improve the quality of future ports. At the very least;
This is by no means an exhaustive list of the problems of the port, but without these fixes for FFXIII and future ports, I don't think I would be able to recomend any of these games to anyone.

Like Gone Home, the previous title of the developers, this is more an immersive story than a "game" in the traditionnal meaning of the word. Tacoma improves on it by bringing innovative game controls : instead of reading papers and looking at clues, you can move around people and conversations in space and time. Characters brightly come to life thanks to great dialogues and voice acting. While we meet each of them for only a short time, they stand out as fully fleshed-out individuals, with their unique personalities, doubts, worries, and quirky traits. As in Gone Home, the environments are crammed with details and contain little jokes or references to other parts of the story.
I wish a few aspects could have been improved, however. The story is not as satisfying as Gone Home. Without going into details to avoid giving spoilers, part of the pleasure in Gone Home was understanding how the stories of father, mother and daughter fit together tragically. How the obvious, recent storyline was the consequence of events started decades ago in the same house. All parts fit together to give the overall story a greater meaning. In Tacoma, this aspect is almost absent. We get a bit of this satisfaction when we discover
However, it is pretty limited in reach. It connects to another person (
). But all other backstories are scattered and unrelated. They do fit together in the theme of unhapiness in the gig economy, but that's not quite as powerful as the Gone Home story. Perhaps I will discover more after other explorations. I'm still not sure of what happened to (
).
Allover I recomment the game. It's a bit short indeed, but well worth the price if you enjoy walking simulators and innovative narration.

This is by far the best Super Meat Boy clone I've ever seen.
Before you read the following text, I want to say that I only have the best attitude to this game and I highly enjoy my stay in it!
-> TL;DR is available at the end of this review;
Why is it SMB-like in my opinion?
1) The same clunky controls with acceleration;
2) Basically the same threats:
- Saw throwers are replaced with triangles;
- Homing rockets are now circles;
- Bosses remind me of SMB a little bit;
- Land Saws are replaced with Spikes;
3) Collect a key - destroy blocks with keyholes type of a thing;
4) Badges are replaced with hearts;
5) Similar walljumps;
What's new?
1) Enemies. They're quite simple and annoying at times to kill because of, again, the SMB-like clunky controls, but they're neat anyways;
2) You can't see the level. Cool! Even though in the end it is still the same platformer, but it looks soooo coool!
3) The graphics and polish level are overwhelmingly awesome! Although it might just repeat the previous statement, I really want to emphasize the fact that this game feels extremely pleasant to play!
4) Double Jump and the cool effect of splatting paint everywhere when you perform it, which allows you to see the level better. Really useful!

In addition to this, INK has great sound effects and an ambient soundtrack!
What's bad?
1) The worst thing from SMB came into INK, too.
The achievements. All of them, except for the 3 last ones, seem to be really easy. Those 3 ones though are "Go through area ??? without dying". This game is just not fun without dying and trying out new stuff, without rushing forwards from a moving platform in order to achieve right timing and all those great things! This is actually not the game where you should see those ones in my humble opinion..
TL:DR
If you look at Super Meat Boy and say something like "Damn! This game is great!" or you're a crazy fan of it just as I am, this is a MUST-HAVE!
Even though I'm on the 57th level a the moment of writing and so far the game was extremely easy, especially the second boss, this game brings the joy I had on my first playthrough of SMB back and allows me to experience it all again!
Thank you, ZackBellGames!

Hi! I`ve read some reviews on this game and I`m amaized on how many positive reviews it has. And most of players have 20+ hrs in this game. Is really ridiculous!!!
Why I find that ridicoulous, I will explain in the following review.
SPOILERS AHEAD!!! TLTR and also game is about to be explained!!!
I want to start by saying I don`t have many hours in game at the moment of this review (only 7 hrs) because I started just today, even tho I previously downloaded the game and after I saw you actually need to make an account to play it, I bailed out.
So, today (16 July 2016) I`ve redownloaded this game, because a friend recommended to me and said it was fun and cool. It was anything but fun or cool.
I have to say that I didn`t really enjoyed it! The fact that you must create an account ONLY inside game and not on their website (there`s no option to make an account on their website nor to log in), extremelly long Training (took me 2 hrs to complete it, with fails), tedious leveling and IP (Infinity Points) grinding, Predictable A.I. and also the fact that if you want to PvP, the game queues you against someone who cleary knows how to wreck you in the first 5 minutes in the game and who cleary spend Real Money to buy packs, you don`t have any option to play against new players! These things are a major turn down for me!!!
Pros:
- Animated cards and battlefield;
- Simultaneous turns for both players or A.I.
- Resources increase automatically so no need to sacrifice any of your monsters or spells to gain more resource;
- Inside match "Trading post", where you have the options to shuffle your hand only 1 per match and get a different hand, draw a card (all of these cost Resouces, of course).
- Beautifull graphics (at least not too bad or shady for my taste) and really nice designed artstyle on cards;
- Ability to farm for card packs and avatars;
- Ability to Trade;
- Ability to gift store items to your friends or other players;
- Ability to challenge your friends to duel you;
- Low resource game (just 1.5 GBs);
- Free to play;
Cons:
- You can create account only inside game and not on their website;
- Long Training (2 hrs took me to complete it). Please name 1 more game where you have to spend 2 Hours in the tutorial because mostly of the fails!!! What tutorial is that where you can actually fail?!?;
- Factions and Lore are not explained (who is against who and why);
- Functions and main Menu are also not explained (how to return to main menu, how to add players as friends, how to ignore players, how to trade with other players, how to duel with your friends/other players, how to change your title/profile picture, basically ending up with default one);
- Leveling and Grinding for XP and IPs is verry long and tedious (at least for me, it kinda feel like a waste of time, like I haven`t accomplished anything in this game);
- No Achivements, Trading Cards or at least some freaking Badges;
- A bug where your cursor is invisible (or disabled) during dialogues (mainly in Tutorial).
Thank you for reading my review!!! I personally don`t really recommend this game but if your curious what`s about feel free to try it and later on, post your own review!!! Have fun!!!

Civ 5 is pretty much my favorite game,beyond earth is another story.Here are just a few rants I come up with:
1)The whole landscape looks freaking gloomy and ugly;
2) tons of bugs,we all saw the explorer-jogging-forever glitch in those preview builds,they say they are going to fix it,well,NO THEY DIDN'T.While all the bugs they did not fix in civ 5,obviously still exists;
3)Extremely imbalanced gameplay,Apollo(highest difficulty) is a cakewalk,no improvements on AI whatsoever;but the most game breaking of all are the internal trade routes,way too powerful;
4)Where are the demographics screen?!!City list?!And global politics screen doesn't even tell you which factions are at war with each other?!If you want to reskin the entire Civ 5,at very least keep all those good stuff,do we have to buy some"Info pack DLC" in the future?Not surprised if they do release it.
5)I understand there will be more factions coming in DLC's,but 8 factions only?!That's just pathetic.
6)Each city can have up to 3 trade routes,each trade route must be manually reassigned every 20 turns,so if you have more than 30 cities in the late game and want to find the best trade route among a long list of cities...enjoy!(unlike in civ 5,there is no "sort by highest gold" option)
7)Almost forgot to mention,they did not even make a single scenario;
Overall,this game is,as everyone said,a reskin of civ 5,but feels extremely rushed and missing so much stuff,just like civ 5 back upon release,I do have faith in Firaxis coming up with great DLCs,but at this state I cannot say this game worth the price.

